#prob(title: "Curtis 4.6")[
    With respect to the geocentric equatorial frame, the position vector of a spacecraft is $vbu(r) = - 6000 vuu(I) - 1000 vuu(J) - 5000 vuu(K) space (unit("km"))$ and the orbit's eccentricity vector is $vbu(e) = -0.4 vuu(I) + -0.5 vuu(J) + -0.6 vuu(K)$. Calculate the true anomaly $theta$ if the satellite is approaching perigee.
] <hwk:p04>

The eccentricity vector points toward perigee, so:
$
    cos(theta)= (vbu(e) dot vbu(r))/(e r).
$

The required magnitudes and dot product are:
$
    e & = sqrt(0.4^2+0.5^2+0.6^2)=#result("04", "e"), \
    r & = sqrt(6000^2+1000^2+5000^2) space unit("km") = qty(result("04", "r"), "km"), \
    vbu(e) dot vbu(r) & = -qty(5900, "km").
$

Thus, we have:
$
            cos(theta) & = #result("04", "cos theta"), \
    arccos(cos(theta)) & = qty(result("04", "principal"), "degree").
$

Unfortunately, the inverse cosine alone does not distinguish the inbound and outbound branches.

Since $v_r=(mu/H)e sin(theta)<0$ when approaching perigee, $theta$ is bounded by $qty(180, "degree")<theta<qty(360, "degree")$.

Therefore, the true anomaly is:
$
    theta & = qty(360, "degree")-qty(result("04", "principal"), "degree") \
          & = qty(result("04", "theta"), "degree") quad qed
$
<hwk:s04>

#pagebreak(weak: true)

#prob(title: "Curtis 4.7")[
    Given that, relative to the geocentric equatorial frame, $vbu(r) = - 6600 vuu(I) - 1300 vuu(J) - 5200 vuu(K) space (unit("km"))$, the eccentricity vector is $vbu(e) = - 0.4 vuu(I) - 0.5 vuu(J) - 0.6 vuu(K)$, and the satellite is flying toward perigee, calculate the inclination of the orbit.
] <hwk:p05>

Both $vbu(r)$ and $vbu(e)$ lie in the orbital plane, so their cross product determines the normal up to its sign.

With $theta$ measured in the direction of motion, the cross product is:
$
    vbu(e) times vbu(r)=e r sin(theta) vuu(H).
$

The satellite is flying toward perigee, so $sin(theta)<0$.

Therefore, the unit angular momentum vector is:
$
    vuu(H)=(vbu(r) times vbu(e))/(abs(vbu(r) times vbu(e))).
$

Computing the cross product gives:
$
    vbu(r) times vbu(e)
    &= det(mat(vuu(I), vuu(J), vuu(K); -6600, -1300, -5200; -0.4, -0.5, -0.6)) \
    &= vecrow(-1820, -1880, 2780) space (unit("km")), \
    abs(vbu(r) times vbu(e))&=qty(result("05", "cross magnitude"), "km").
$

The inclination is the angle between $vuu(H)$ and $vuu(K)$:
$
    cos(i)=vuu(H) dot vuu(K)
    =2780/sqrt(1820^2+1880^2+2780^2)=#result("05", "cos i").
$

Thus, the inclination of the orbit is:
$
    i=arccos(#result("05", "cos i"))=qty(result("05", "i"), "degree") quad qed
$
<hwk:s05>

