#prob(title: "")[
    Find the equinoctial elements for the following orbits:

    + Position and Velocity:
        $
            vbu(r) = mat(3698.3455; -34232.4473; 0) space (unit("km")), quad
            vbu(v) = mat(2.1482; -1.4944; 0) space (unit("km/s"))
        $
    <hwk:p06a>

    + Position and Velocity:
        $
            vbu(r) = mat(-21082.0848; 36515.242; 0) space (unit("km")), quad
            vbu(v) = mat(-2.6627; -1.5373; 0) space (unit("km/s"))
        $
    <hwk:p06b>

    + Position and Velocity:
        $
            vbu(r) = mat(-15843.4562; -2247.7766; 21200.4627) space (unit("km")), quad
            vbu(v) = mat(1.4544; -3.5188; 0.7138) space (unit("km/s"))
        $
    <hwk:p06c>
] <hwk:p06>

+ The orbit is equatorial and prograde because $H_x=H_y=0$ and $H_z>0$.

    Thus, $i=0$ and $p=q=0$.

    Although the ascending node is undefined, the longitude of perigee $pi.alt=Omega+omega$ is well defined for this eccentric orbit.

    In the equatorial plane, the eccentricity vector gives $k=e_x$ and $h=e_y$:
    $
        vbu(e) & = vecrow(-0.3623940235, 0.6276769176, 0), \
             e & = #result("06a", "e").
    $

    We can obtain the true longitude directly from position via:
    $
        L = theta + omega + Omega = op("atan2")(r_y,r_x)=qty(result("06a", "L"), "degree").
    $

    We now require the equinoctial eccentric longitude and the parameter $beta$:
    $
           F & = pi.alt+E \
        beta & = 1/(1+sqrt(1-h^2-k^2)).
    $

    With $1/beta=1+sqrt(1-h^2-k^2)$, the eccentric longitude is:
    $
        F & = L+2op("atan2")(h cos(L)-k sin(L),1/beta+k cos(L)+h sin(L)).
    $ <eqn:longitude>

    Recall that $lambda=pi.alt+E-e sin(E)$, meaning:
    $
        lambda & = F+h cos(F)-k sin(F).
    $

    After reducing $lambda mod 2pi$, the equinoctial elements are:
    $
             a & = qty(result("06a", "a"), "km"), \
             h & = #result("06a", "h"), \
             k & = #result("06a", "k"), \
             p & = 0, \
             q & = 0, \
        lambda & = qty(result("06a", "lambda"), "degree") quad qed
    $
<hwk:s06a>

+ Again, $H_x=H_y=0$ and $H_z>0$, so $p=q=0$, $h=e_y$, and $k=e_x$.

    Computing directly from the given state gives:
    $
        e & = #result("06b", "e") \
        L & = qty(result("06b", "L"), "degree").
    $

    This orbit is nearly circular. Retaining the small eccentricity from the supplied rounded state, and using #link(<eqn:longitude>)[the longitude equations], gives:
    $
             a & = qty(result("06b", "a"), "km"), \
             h & = #result("06b", "h"), \
             k & = #result("06b", "k"), \
             p & = 0, \
             q & = 0, \
        lambda & = qty(result("06b", "lambda"), "degree") quad qed
    $

    In the circular limit, $h=k=0$ and $lambda=L$, so no perigee direction is needed.
<hwk:s06b>

+ For an inclined orbit, we obtain $p$ and $q$ directly from angular momentum:
    $
        p=H_x/(H+H_z), quad q=-H_y/(H+H_z).
    $

    Let $s=1+p^2+q^2$ and introduce the orthonormal axes in the orbital plane
    $
        vuu(f) & =1/s vecrow(1-p^2+q^2, 2p q, -2p), \
        vuu(g) & =1/s vecrow(2p q, 1+p^2-q^2, 2q).
    $

    These reduce to $vuu(I)$ and $vuu(J)$ when $p=q=0$. In this basis,
    $
        k & = vbu(e) dot vuu(f), \
        h & = vbu(e) dot vuu(g), \
        L & = op("atan2")(vbu(r) dot vuu(g), vbu(r) dot vuu(f)).
    $
    Substitution gives:
    $
        vbu(H) & =vecrow(72995.725212, 42143.011986, 59019.119964) space (unit("km^2/s")), \
        e & =#result("06c", "e"), \
        L&=qty(result("06c", "L"), "degree").
    $

    Using #link(<eqn:longitude>)[the longitude equations] and the energy relation for $a$, the equinoctial elements are:
    $
             a & = qty(result("06c", "a"), "km"), \
             h & = #result("06c", "h"), \
             k & = #result("06c", "k"), \
             p & = #result("06c", "p"), \
             q & = #result("06c", "q"), \
        lambda & = qty(result("06c", "lambda"), "degree") quad qed
    $
    This orbit is also nearly circular. The small nonzero values of $h$ and $k$ are retained rather than setting them to zero.
<hwk:s06c>

